 <details><summary>Historical registration-size experiment</summary><div class="detail-body"><section id="packed"><div class="section-head"><div><div class="eyebrow">03 / Registration granularity</div><h2>Small tables have a large footprint</h2><p>The same million rows, split into registrations of 1, 4 or 16 rows. Each registration contains one table and borrows one of six shared prototypes.</p></div><span class="section-tag">Data · later memory run</span></div><div class="split"><div class="panel"><div class="controls"><label>Overridden rows<select id="packed-density"><option value="10">10% · 100,000 rows</option><option value="1">1% · 10,000 rows</option><option value="0">0% · defaults only</option></select></label></div><div class="chart"><div class="legend"><span class="legend-item" style="--color:#286761">Loaded registrations</span><span class="legend-item" style="--color:#55a89c">Two query caches</span><span class="legend-item" style="--color:#c0ddcc">Override storage</span></div><div id="packed-bars" aria-live="polite"></div><p class="chart-note">One common linear scale across group sizes. Totals include store metadata, row identities, query bindings and override arenas. Shared source blobs are counted separately.</p></div></div><aside class="aside-note"><div class="eyebrow">One-row registrations</div><div class="big" id="packed-blocks"></div><p>live heap blocks at the selected override density.</p><hr><h3>Query bookkeeping is visible too</h3><p>Creating the two queries adds <strong id="query-allocations"></strong> heap allocations and <strong id="query-bytes"></strong> for one-row tables, before overrides.</p><p>Reset releases override arenas. Unloading registrations retains store capacity until final destruction.</p></aside></div><div class="panel" style="margin-top:18px"><div class="table-scroll" id="packed-table"></div></div></section></div></details>
-<section id="method"><div class="section-head"><div><div class="eyebrow">04 / Reading the comparison</div><h2>Same logical workload, different contracts</h2></div></div><div class="cards"><article class="card" style="--color:var(--data)"><h3>Defold</h3><p>Fixed-stride mutable rows with shared property metadata. Resource loading borrows the original blob; instance creation appends fixed row bytes to dense tables shared by registrations of that resource. Stable registration handles and row membership indices use pooled slots. Numeric/math writes access these existing bytes. Strings and dynamic containers initially share default payloads; replacements belong to each registration (or table for independently added rows). Reset copies loaded or added defaults back.</p><p>Nested Light is an inline struct. Public iteration holds a structural lock and relies on caller-owned cursor lifetimes. Value writes remain allowed; callers collect deletion IDs and apply them after unlocking. Inline row/field cursors advance indices; typed getters resolve addresses and read shared bindings on access. Type/name metadata accessors avoid copying metadata per row. A light.color query binds the complete member offset and kind once per table. Queries resolve full property paths and kinds once at creation through DataQueryFindField, then reuse the handles across batches and traversals through public typed field pointers. Explosion requests a writable health pointer only for hit rows. Field order is unspecified. Metadata is shared across rows. Dynamic structs and lists remain supported separately.</p></article><article class="card" style="--color:var(--columns)"><h3>Flecs</h3><p>Position, Health, Velocity and Light are separate components, with concrete-type tags and the same logical identity data.</p><p>Queries iterate component columns and access native values directly. This benchmark mutates current values in place. Flecs also supports <a href="https://www.flecs.dev/flecs/PrefabsManual.html">shared prefab defaults and reverting inherited component overrides</a>, which are not measured here.</p></article><article class="card" style="--color:#387fba"><h3>Bevy</h3><p>The standalone ECS crate uses separate Position, Health, Velocity and Light components with default table storage and normal change tracking.</p><p>Queries access typed components directly. No renderer or scheduler is included. This benchmark restores values from fixture data between cases; <a href="https://docs.rs/bevy_ecs/latest/bevy_ecs/change_detection/struct.Mut.html">ordinary component mutation</a> does not retain an initial-value baseline.</p></article></div><p class="small muted" style="margin-top:18px">Complete-struct variants store one native component per concrete type, with inline Light fields and a typed adapter for shared fields. These additional layout measurements remain in detailed tables and source data.</p><div class="methods"><div><h3>Workload and timing</h3><ul><li>Deterministic seed <code>0x12345678</code>. Backend order rotates; the warmup sample is discarded.</li><li>Read cases use five warm passes. First and repeat writes use one pass each.</li><li>The Explosion case scans 500,000 health/position candidates at 1M population, subtracting 25 health within radius 50 of the origin, clamped at zero. Shuffled position visits each instance once and increments position X by one. Both start from reset defaults; no spatial index is used.</li><li>Fixture generation, validation and between-case reset are outside timing. Insertions include allocation and live-query maintenance.</li><li>Packed cases repeat each prototype’s values. The main comparison uses 16 rows per logical registration, stored in six shared Defold tables.</li></ul></div><div><h3>Memory accounting and limits</h3><ul><li>C++ memory runs intercept ordinary allocation/deallocation and Flecs OS callbacks. Bevy memory runs wrap the Rust global allocator. Timing binaries have no tracking hooks.</li><li>Live bytes include unused library capacity. Peaks begin at operation entry; resize peaks exclude the allocator’s transient internal copy.</li><li>Allocation counts refer to heap calls, not values carved from arenas or Flecs pools. Request counts add allocation and resize calls within each sample.</li><li>Fixture inputs, output-ID arrays and shared Data blob assets are recorded separately. They are excluded from library charts.</li><li>Allocator/tracker headers, allocator rounding, stack/static storage, platform/libc internals and process RSS are excluded. CPU model was not recorded.</li></ul></div></div><details><summary>Validation and remaining coverage</summary><div class="detail-body small"><p>Every measured run checks values, query counts, damage, reset and stale IDs. Memory runs require zero tracked backend allocations at teardown and zero fixture/resource allocations at exit. The allocator self-test checks allocation and resize arithmetic, ownership, calloc, realloc, strdup and dlib array growth. C20 passes 36 tests / 21,606 assertions in Release and ASAN, including pooled registration reuse, live queries across capacity growth, independent reset/unload after row removal and slot reuse, immutable resources and shared nested payloads. All 1,344 paired C19/C20 samples match operation counts and checksums; table-batch counts intentionally differ. All C++ dense/packed timing and memory workloads pass ASAN. SDK docs/examples validate; all 896 Bevy/Flecs samples agree. Explosion, shuffled position and packed scalar writes assert zero allocation requests and zero heap growth. File loading retains five/three requests across all 56 cases in both builds.</p><p style="margin-top:12px">Historical timing validation: 16 tests / 3,761 assertions. Historical memory: 17 tests / 3,873 assertions. E3 experiment: 18 tests / 8,208 assertions in Release and ASAN, plus ASAN memory workloads at 1/4/16 packed rows. E6: 19 tests / 8,858 assertions, ASAN decoded and 1/4/16-row packed memory workloads, and the 5/3 file-loading allocation budget. C5 math reads: 20 tests / 8,929 assertions passed in Release and ASAN. Historical C18 query field handles: 34 tests / 12,202 assertions pass in Release and ASAN, including handles obtained before matching tables exist and reused after table removal/re-registration with different layouts. Historical C17 aligned field pointers: 33 tests / 12,133 assertions pass in Release and ASAN. All C++ dense/packed timing and memory workloads pass ASAN; SDK documentation/examples validate. All 896 Bevy/Flecs and 630 paired C16/C17 samples agree on counts/checksums. Both builds pass 56 file-load cases with five/three allocation requests. Historical C16 single-field light queries: 30 tests / 11,946 assertions pass in Release and ASAN; all C++ dense/packed timing and memory workloads pass ASAN, all 896 Bevy/Flecs samples agree, and all 40 Defold heap phases match C15. Historical C15 separate workload functions: 30 tests / 11,946 assertions pass in Release and ASAN, including field selection by name with both descriptor orders in decoded and packed data. All C++ dense/packed timing and memory workloads and both profiling modes pass ASAN. SDK docs/examples validate; all 896 Bevy/Flecs samples agree, and all 40 Defold heap phases match C14. Historical C14 inline typed getters: 30 tests / 11,914 assertions pass in Release and ASAN, including C callbacks and absent-field output preservation for all six getters. SDK docs/examples validate; all 896 Bevy/Flecs samples agree and all 40 Defold heap phases match C13. Historical C13 direct field cursors: 30 tests / 11,908 assertions pass in Release and ASAN, including owner-filtered batches after swap removal. SDK docs/examples validate; all 896 Bevy/Flecs samples agree and all 40 Defold heap phases match C12. Historical C12 index cursors: 29 tests / 11,847 assertions pass in Release and ASAN; C-only SDK examples and metadata accessors validate, all 896 Bevy/Flecs samples agree, and all 40 Defold heap phases match C11. Historical C11 locked iteration: 29 tests / 11,799 assertions pass in Release and ASAN, including blocked structural mutations, nested locks, C-only SDK, writes/reset, deferred stable-ID removals and reusable buffers. All 896 Bevy/Flecs samples agree; 56 file-load cases in each build retain the five/three allocation budget. Historical C10 compact field cursors: 30 tests / 10,254 assertions pass in Release and ASAN, including preserved parent/store invalidation and unfiltered/inline container metadata. All 896 Bevy/Flecs samples agree; 56 file-load cases retain the five/three allocation budget. Historical C9 public row/field cursors: 29 tests / 10,053 assertions pass in Release and ASAN, including C-only SDK, private-member documentation validation, unfiltered enumeration, independent snapshots, reset and stale cursor tests. All 896 Bevy/Flecs samples agree; all 56 file-load cases pass the five/three allocation budget. C8 cached batches and typed specialization: 26 tests / 9,786 assertions pass in Release and ASAN; 896 Bevy/Flecs samples agree and all 56 file-load cases keep the five/three allocation budget. C7 inline composition: 24 tests / 9,442 assertions pass in Release and ASAN, including fixed member layouts, copied query paths, nested writes/reset, packed roundtrips and malformed metadata. Small runtime-created and packed memory runs pass under ASAN; fresh/reused file loads retain the 5/3 allocation budget. C6 public typed reads: 21 tests / 9,005 assertions pass in Release and ASAN, including C-only SDK compilation, unaligned nested reads, float-bit preservation, changing member layouts, overrides/reset and error handling. Small runtime-created and 16-row packed memory workloads also pass under ASAN. Remaining variants include 50%/100% overrides, distinct source blobs, multi-type registrations, short-string churn, additional insertion patterns, process memory and controlled cold-cache runs. C1 adds standalone Bevy ECS execution: 1,988 operation samples match Flecs counts, hit counts and checksums. Rust layout and allocator tests pass in Release; no Rust ASAN run is included. No Unity execution or Bob/gamesys integration is included.</p></div></details></section>
